            <ArticleTitle>Effect of cervical mobilization and ischemic compression therapy on contralateral cervical side flexion and pressure pain threshold in latent upper trapezius trigger points.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>477-83</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.jbmt.2015.11.010</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1360-8592(15)00280-6</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Studies have shown a clinical relationship between trigger points and joint impairments. However the cause-and effect relationship between muscle and joint dysfunctions in trigger points could not be established. The purpose of this study was to investigate effects of mobilization and ischemic compression therapy on cervical range of motion and pressure pain sensitivity in participants with latent trigger point in the upper trapezius muscle. Ninety asymptomatic participants with upper trapezius latent trigger point were randomized in to 3 groups: mobilization, ischemic compression and a control. The outcomes were measured over a 2 week period. Repeated measures ANOVA showed statistically and clinically significant pre to post improvement in both the interventional groups compared to control (p &lt; 0.05). However the effect sizes between the intervention groups were small (&lt;0.3) revealing minimal clinical detectable difference.</AbstractText>
